  "units": "outputs: annual site energy in kWh (published as integer kBtu x 0.29307107); inputs: ft2, year, degF, ACH50, R-value (h ft2 F/Btu), %, kWdc, degrees lat/lon, AFUE %, HSPF/SEER/EER, 0/1 flags and one-hot indicators",
  "generation": "States AZ, CA, CO, CT, FL, GA, IL, MA, ME, MI, MN, NC, NH, NJ, NY, PA, TX, VA, WA, WI; baseline (upgrade 0) models with completed_status Success; 2500 buildings per state drawn without replacement (numpy default_rng(2025), states in the order listed, rows sorted by bldg_id), n = 50000; sampling is equal per state, not by ResStock weight. Numeric inputs are parsed from characteristic labels (vintage decade -> midpoint year, setpoints, R-values, window-to-wall %, AFUE/HSPF/SEER/EER, usage %, flags for appliances present); categoricals are one-hot over the values present in the sample. No out.* column is used as input. End-use columns that are zero in every sampled building are dropped (fuel oil / gas / propane heat-pump backup). End uses per fuel: {'electricity': 27, 'natural_gas': 9, 'fuel_oil': 2, 'propane': 4}. Values are independently rounded integer kBtu, so the four end-use identities hold to a few kBtu (rounding; the harness projects labels) and the site-total and net identities hold exactly (wood and other fuels are not in the site total in this release). Max relative residual per end-use row on this sample: electricity 5.5e-5, gas 2.6e-5, fuel oil 4.4e-5, propane 9.7e-5 (close to the 1e-4 projection limit; re-check the gate if the sample changes). Group split by state.",
  "x_desc": "119 building characteristics: 48 numeric (floor area, vintage, stories, bedrooms, occupants, setpoints, envelope, HVAC and water-heater efficiency, appliance flags, location) and one-hot heating / water-heater fuel, dryer, range, building type, heating / cooling type, climate zone, foundation, lighting, spa and pool-heater fuel",
